{
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "metadata": {
    "note": "The Maine Central's Mountain Division, built by the Portland and Ogdensburg Railroad and completed through Crawford Notch in 1875, was the railroad that opened the eastern White Mountains (Conway, North Conway, Bartlett) to tourism from Portland -- a gap in this map's New Hampshire coverage before now, distinct from the Boston, Concord and Montreal Railroad's approach to the White Mountains from the south/west (see boston_concord_montreal_railroad.geojson). The 131-mile line ran Portland, ME through Fryeburg, Conway, North Conway, Bartlett, and the Crawford Notch pass, then west through Fabyan (crossing the Boston and Maine's Cog Railway branch), Twin Mountain, and Whitefield, across the Connecticut River valley to St. Johnsbury, VT. Leased to the Maine Central in 1888; abandoned in 1983, though the Conway Scenic Railroad has run heritage excursions on the surviving eastern portion (North Conway through Crawford Notch to Fabyan) since 1995. Geometry is real-traced from OpenStreetMap (active rail combined with abandoned/disused railway tagging); every hop resolved to a connected, plausible real track path with no straight-line fallbacks needed.",
    "sources": [
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain_Division (full station list with mileposts, Portland MP0 to St. Johnsbury MP131.4, via Fryeburg, Conway Center, North Conway, Bartlett, Notchland/Bemis, Crawford Notch, Fabyan, Twin Mountain, Whitefield, Lunenburg VT, Concord VT; line abandoned 1983)",
      "search results citing: Portland & Ogdensburg Railroad chartered 1867, reorganized as Portland & Ogdensburg Railway 1886, leased to Maine Central Railroad 1888; Crawford Notch crossing completed and opened 1875; Fabyan crossing point with the B&M's Mount Washington Cog Railway branch to Marshfield; Conway Scenic Railroad heritage operation since 1995"
    ],
    "geometry_sources": [
      "OpenStreetMap (via Overpass API, overpass.openstreetmap.fr mirror): railway=rail (active, including the operating Conway Scenic Railroad heritage trackage) combined with railway=abandoned/disused tagging, Portland ME to St. Johnsbury VT; routed via Dijkstra shortest path between each station's geocoded coordinates. All hops resolved to connected, plausible paths with no fallbacks needed."
    ],
    "not_yet_sourced": "Exact frequency/timetable for any era; precise dates for individual station openings versus the 1875 Crawford Notch completion date.",
    "pulled": "20260824",
    "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."
  },
